  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread rain continues this evening and overnight \nwith some urban and poor drainage flooding possible. Rain will be \nheavy at times southeast MA and RI where flood risk is greatest. \n\nOur front has arrived and stalled as expected, now draped generally \nfrom New London, CT to Lawrence, MA. This, as a mid level trough \ndigs into the northeast. As it does it will continue to take on a \nmore neutral tilt keeping flow parallel to the front and extending \nthe ongoing training of moderate to heavy rain focused on southeast \nMA. Impressive dynamics from placement beneath the RRQ of a 100+kt \nupper jet and a 40-45 kt LLJ will continue to act on copious \nmoisture (PWATs 3+ SD above normal) to drop another 1-3 inches on \nsoutheast MA through sunrise Saturday. In addition to the 1-2.5 \ninches that fell through mid afternoon this had led to some \nscattered reports of flooding in the Falmouth area and prompted a \nfew Flood Warnings. This fire hose of moisture is progged to \ncontinue focusing on the same area through the overnight hours \nbefore the front finally moves east as another surface low forms \nalong the front and strengthens over the Gulf of Maine. The column \ndries quite a bit on Saturday bringing widespread heavy rain to an \nend, but the upper trough lingers overhead Saturday and Saturday \nnight. This will mean a mix of clouds and sun on Saturday with \nincreasing coverage of scattered showers thanks to increased lapse \nrates under the cold pool. Temperature-wise the airmass will be \nstarting to moderate from the extreme warmth/humidity of late with \ndewpoints dropping behind the front (especially late in the day) and \nhighs in the low 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather continues into this weekend, \nthen cooler and drier conditions arrive next week.\n\nIn the wake of today's system, surface low pressure develops in\nthe Gulf of Maine Saturday night into Sunday morning. This low \nshifts off to the north and east through Sunday morning while \nthe core of an upper level trough steadily follows it from the \nwest. This core is expected to move over southern New England \nover the same time period, and with it, much cooler temperatures\naloft. Temperatures at 500 mb dip to around -20C, with some \nguidance falling to -25C. Closer to the surface, temperatures at\n925 mb fall to around 2-4C and temperatures at 850 mb fall to \naround 0C by early Sunday morning. Once the upper level trough \nclears off to the north and east, 850 mb temperatures improve \nslightly to just around 2C. This colder air aloft will be more \nsupportive of highs Sunday into the start of next week falling \nback to the 50s with cool nights in the 30s and 40s.\n\nNAEFS guidance indicates no significant deviations from the  \nclimatological percentiles for temperatures or moisture for the\nnext week, though did note 90th percentile zonal winds aloft \nMonday into Wednesday across much of the region. Persistent \nwesterly flow with notably breezy conditions Monday and Tuesday \nis expected. Winds generally stay westerly through much of the \nweek, given the series of fronts expected to move through the \nregion. With the drier conditions (PWATS to around 0.5\" and less\nand dew points back in the 30s and 40s) at the surface, not \nmuch in the way of precipitation is expected with these frontal \npassages; just cooler air. Occasional cloudiness is expected at \nmost with passing moist air aloft.",
